Absolutely! Here's a **professional-looking LaTeX TikZ/pgfplots** code that creates a 2D plot with the following features:

- A point labeled $x$.
- Three other points labeled $y_1$, $y_2$, and $y_3$ that **dominate** $x$ (i.e., they are to the **top-right** of $x$).
- The **region to the top-right of $x$** is shaded in **pale red**.
- **Gray dashed axis-parallel lines** go through $x$.
- The plot is styled for **professional print output** (e.g., in a book).

---

### ✅ LaTeX Code

```latex
\documentclass{article}
\usepackage{pgfplots}
\pgfplotsset{compat=1.18}
\usetikzlibrary{patterns, arrows.meta}

% Define professional colors
\definecolor{pale_red}{RGB}{255, 204, 204}
\definecolor{accent_blue}{RGB}{0, 102, 153}
\definecolor{gray_dashed}{RGB}{160, 160, 160}

\begin{document}

\begin{tikzpicture}
\begin{axis}[
    axis lines = middle,
    xlabel = $x_1$,
    ylabel = $x_2$,
    xmin = 0, xmax = 10,
    ymin = 0, ymax = 10,
    xtick = \empty,
    ytick = \empty,
    grid = none,
    width = 10cm,
    height = 10cm,
    clip = false,
    every axis x label/.style={at={(current axis.right of origin)}, anchor=west, font=\small},
    every axis y label/.style={at={(current axis.above origin)}, anchor=south, font=\small},
    legend style={font=\small, at={(0.98,0.98)}, anchor=north east}
]

% Coordinates
\def\xx{3}
\def\yy{4}

% Point x
\addplot[only marks, mark=*, mark size=2.5pt, color=accent_blue] coordinates {(\xx,\yy)};
\node[anchor=north east, font=\small] at (\xx,\yy) {$x$};

% Points y1, y2, y3 (dominate x)
\addplot[only marks, mark=square*, mark size=2.5pt, color=accent_blue] coordinates {(5,6) (6,5) (7,7)};
\node[anchor=south west, font=\small] at (5,6) {$y_1$};
\node[anchor=south west, font=\small] at (6,5) {$y_2$};
\node[anchor=south west, font=\small] at (7,7) {$y_3$};

% Shaded region to the top-right of x
\addplot[pattern=north east lines, pattern color=pale_red, opacity=0.5] fill between[
    of = A and B,
    soft clip={domain=3:10, domain y=4:10}
];

% Dashed lines through x
\draw[gray_dashed, dashed] (\xx,0) -- (\xx,10); % vertical
\draw[gray_dashed, dashed] (0,\yy) -- (10,\yy); % horizontal

% Optional: Define helper coordinates for shading
\addplot[name path=A, draw=none] coordinates {(3,4) (10,4)};
\addplot[name path=B, draw=none] coordinates {(3,4) (3,10)};

\end{axis}
\end{tikzpicture}

\end{document}
```

---

### 📝 Notes

- **Color palette** is chosen for print: pale red for shading, a professional blue for points, and gray for dashed lines.
- **Dashed lines** are axis-parallel and go through the point $x$.
- The **shaded region** is to the **top-right** of $x$, using `fill between` and `soft clip`.
- The **legend** is optional and can be removed if not needed.
- The **font size** is set to `\small` for a clean, professional look in a printed book.

---

### 🖨️ Output

This will produce a clean, professional 2D plot suitable for inclusion in a printed academic or technical book. You can adjust the coordinates of the points or the axis limits as needed for your specific use case.

